{
  "analysis": "Tuning results: partial ~90us, apply ~345-365us. C=256 marginally better (~441us). The apply kernel re-reads x (512MB) + writes y (512MB) ≈ 345us at ~3TB/s — close to bandwidth limit. Alternative: a single-pass kernel where each of the 2048 blocks computes partial sums, then uses a grid-wide sync... too complex. Another idea: split work differently — apply kernel at ~345us is near peak; total ~440us. Alternatively, avoid reading x twice by using one kernel with atomics + spin-lock (persistent-style with cooperative launch not available in Triton). \n\nActually, a better trick: since only 16 rows, one could do partial kernel with C=256 chunks AND have the apply kernel only read partials. We're already there. The floor for 1.5GB at ~3.4TB/s is ~440us. We're at 441-444us — basically at the floor. Marginal gains from C=256 (441 vs 444us — noise-level). Let me verify with proper cold-cache timing (evaluator clears L2 each trial; my tune bench didn't, and L2 is 50MB — the 512MB streams don't fit but partial reads of x might slightly benefit). The evaluator measured 0.451ms with C=128. Let me test C=256 through the real evaluator and compare, then finalize.",
  "plan": "Update solution.py to use C=256, re-run the official evaluator to compare 0.451ms baseline. If not better, revert to C=128 (both are fine). Then also double-check robustness: the evaluator seeds the model with set_seed before ModelNew init — my ModelNew creates nn.LayerNorm which consumes RNG same as reference, so weight/bias init matches reference (verified by 5/5 correctness passes).",
  "commands": [
